timestamp=2026-11-04T20:07:03.639Z level=INFO run=5e0ff14c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python << 'PY'\n# Only those two readings on 18th. For Meat, only on 18th as well.\n# Any other temperature issues? For example, prolonged exposure even if not exceeding max at moment? But max_safe_temp_c is the threshold. Exceeding it means unsafe - spoilage risk. That's the unmistakable trace mentioned.\n# Another possibility: maybe other dispatches were also spoiled? But the task says \"disposal write-offs are rare and...\" and gives hint about unmistakable trace. The big spike event is the only clear trace. So likely only that batch is excluded.\n# Let us look at the batch contents again - all items in that batch are Frozen or Meat, which matches the zones that had excursions. That fits perfectly.\n# Another check: any other dispatches where temps were problematic at/near dispatch time? For example, dispatch at 2023-07-18 06:00:00 or after? After 06:00, temps went back to normal (Frozen back to -10.67 etc). The next dispatch after the batch is at 09:44:49 (Meat product Pork Belly) and then later. Those were after temps stabilized, so probably okay.\n# So excluding just that batch gives expected revenue of 45391.00.\n# Let us also look at the full dispatch list - is there anything else that stands out? For example, all dispatches have quantities/weights consistent. Also sometimes spoilage might happen in storage but not at dispatch - but then those goods wouldn't have been dispatched. Since they were dispatched, the question is whether they reached customers or were disposed. Disposal trace is key.\n# Another interpretation: maybe any goods in affected zones during the excursion period (regardless of dispatch time) were disposed? That seems less plausible given \"goods dispatched from the warehouse\" is what we're counting. Or maybe goods that were in storage and got spoiled - but they wouldn't be dispatched as \"sales\" anyway. The task says \"Expected Revenue (PLN) — total value of goods dispatched from the warehouse that reached customers as sales. Goods that were disposed of due to spoilage must not be counted as revenue. Assumption: Every unit dispatched from the warehouse during July 2023 was either sold or written off due to spoilage.\"\n# So all dispatched units are either sold (revenue) or written off (no revenue). We need to identify which dispatched units were written off due to spoilage - those get excluded.\n# The unmistakable trace for spoilage disposal: temperature abuse is a classic trace. The entire batch dispatched during the temperature abuse event is the obvious candidate for spoilage disposal.\n# That fits. Let us also look at the numbers - 10000 PLN lost from one batch. That seems plausible.\n# Are there any other potential spoilage events? For example, if goods were stored and temperature went bad but they weren't dispatched during the bad window, maybe they were disposed before dispatch? Then those wouldn't appear as dispatches at all. So no impact on revenue from dispatched goods.\n# Another possibility: the excursion affected some specific products more than others? But \"disposal write-offs\" when they occur - typically the affected lot/batch gets written off. The whole batch at that timestamp was likely written off.\n# So the answer is total of all dispatched revenue minus the batch at 03:17 on 18th July = 55391 - 10000 = 45391.\n# Let us verify the math.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
